NAME
       ss - show network sockets (listening ports and connections)

SYNOPSIS
       ss [OPTIONS] [FILTER]

DESCRIPTION
       ss lists the sockets of the system: the ports that services listen on
       and the connections that are open. It replaces the older netstat
       command. Use it to check that a service really listens on the port you
       expect before you look at the firewall or SELinux.

       Each line shows the protocol (Netid), the state (LISTEN for a server
       waiting for clients, ESTAB for an open connection, UNCONN for UDP),
       the queue sizes, and the local and remote address with port. An
       address of 0.0.0.0, [::] or * means "every address of this machine".

OPTIONS
       -t, --tcp
              Show TCP sockets.

       -u, --udp
              Show UDP sockets.

       -l, --listening
              Show only listening sockets (servers). Without -l, ss shows open
              connections and leaves listening sockets out.

       -n, --numeric
              Show port numbers instead of service names (22 instead of ssh).

       -p, --processes
              Show the program that owns each socket, with its PID. Only root
              sees other users' programs.

       -a, --all
              Show listening and non-listening sockets.

       -s, --summary
              Print counts of sockets by kind.

       -4, -6 Only IPv4 or only IPv6 sockets.

EXAMPLES
       List all TCP ports that services listen on, with their programs:

           ss -tlnp

       Check whether anything listens on port 82:

           ss -tln | grep ':82 '

       List UDP sockets, such as chronyd's port 323:

           ss -ulnp

NOTES
       The letters are often combined: ss -tulpn shows listening TCP and UDP
       sockets with numbers and programs.

SEE ALSO
       ip(8), firewall-cmd(1), systemctl(1)
